steps.body:
An interval has a start and an end, here `type Interval struct{ Start, End int }`. Checking every pair for overlaps is O(n * n). Sort by start instead, and any overlap is with the block you're building: once an interval starts after that block ends, every later one starts later still, so the block is finished.

So sort with `sort.Slice` and walk once. Each interval either grows the last merged block or starts a new one:

```go harness
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

type Interval struct{ Start, End int }

func main() {
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
ivs := []Interval{{8, 10}, {1, 3}, {2, 6}, {6, 7}, {3, 4}}
sort.Slice(ivs, func(i, j int) bool { return ivs[i].Start < ivs[j].Start })
var merged []Interval
for _, iv := range ivs {
	last := len(merged) - 1
	if last >= 0 && iv.Start <= merged[last].End { // overlaps or touches
		if iv.End > merged[last].End {
			merged[last].End = iv.End // grow the block
		}
	} else {
		merged = append(merged, iv) // a gap: start a new block
	}
}
fmt.Println(merged) // [{1 7} {8 10}]
```

`iv.Start <= merged[last].End` merges intervals that only touch, like `{2, 6}` and `{6, 7}`. Write `<` when touching intervals should stay apart. Keep the bigger end, because an interval inside the block, like `{3, 4}`, must not shrink it. Change the block through `merged[last]`: a copy such as `b := merged[last]` would grow only `b`.

The sort is O(n log n) and the walk is O(n), plus O(n) space for `merged`.

