steps.body:
When the intervals are already sorted by start and don't overlap, adding one more needs no sort. Their ends are then sorted too, so the list splits into three runs, in order: the intervals that end before the new one starts, the ones that overlap it, and the ones that start after it ends. Walk them with one index, here with `type Interval struct{ Start, End int }` and the new interval in `add`:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

type Interval struct{ Start, End int }

func main() {
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
ivs := []Interval{{1, 2}, {4, 6}, {8, 10}, {12, 15}}
add := Interval{5, 9}
var out []Interval
i := 0
for i < len(ivs) && ivs[i].End < add.Start { // ends before add: keep it
	out = append(out, ivs[i])
	i++
}
for i < len(ivs) && ivs[i].Start <= add.End { // overlaps add: swallow it
	if ivs[i].Start < add.Start {
		add.Start = ivs[i].Start
	}
	if ivs[i].End > add.End {
		add.End = ivs[i].End
	}
	i++
}
out = append(out, add)
out = append(out, ivs[i:]...) // the rest start after add ends
fmt.Println(out)              // [{1 2} {4 10} {12 15}]
```

The second loop grows `add` over every interval it overlaps, taking the smaller start and the bigger end. Both conditions count touching as overlapping, so an interval that ends exactly where `add` starts is joined. To keep touching intervals apart, use `<=` in the first loop and `<` in the second.

`i` only moves forward, so the insert is O(n) time, plus O(n) for the new slice. Sorting and merging again would cost O(n log n).

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

type Interval struct{ Start, End int }

func insert(ivs []Interval, add Interval) []Interval {
	var out []Interval
	i := 0
	for i < len(ivs) && ivs[i].End < add.Start {
		out = append(out, ivs[i])
		i++
	}
	for i < len(ivs) && ivs[i].Start <= add.End {
		if ivs[i].Start < add.Start {
			add.Start = ivs[i].Start
		}
		if ivs[i].End > add.End {
			add.End = ivs[i].End
		}
		i++
	}
	out = append(out, add)
	return append(out, ivs[i:]...)
}

func main() {
	var n int
	fmt.Scan(&n)
	marks := make([]Interval, n)
	for i := range marks {
		fmt.Scan(&marks[i].Start, &marks[i].End)
	}
	var h Interval
	fmt.Scan(&h.Start, &h.End)

	marks = insert(marks, h)
	for _, m := range marks {
		fmt.Printf("%d to %d\n", m.Start, m.End)
	}
	fmt.Println("highlights:", len(marks))
}

