}

steps.body:
To remove the fewest intervals so the rest don't overlap, keep as many as you can. The greedy rule: sort by **end**, then keep each interval that starts at or after the end of the last one kept. Everything else is removed.

Why the earliest end? Put it in place of the first interval of any best answer: it ends no later, so it can't clash with the rest, and keeping it never costs you. Sorting by start fails: `{1, 10}` starts first, but keeping it blocks both `{2, 3}` and `{4, 5}`. With `type Interval struct{ Start, End int }`, the walk needs only a count and the last kept end:

```go harness
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
)

type Interval struct{ Start, End int }

func main() {
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
ivs := []Interval{{1, 10}, {2, 3}, {4, 5}, {3, 4}}
sort.Slice(ivs, func(i, j int) bool { return ivs[i].End < ivs[j].End })
kept, lastEnd := 0, 0
for _, iv := range ivs {
	if kept == 0 || iv.Start >= lastEnd { // fits after the last one kept
		kept++
		lastEnd = iv.End
	}
}
fmt.Println("keep", kept, "remove", len(ivs)-kept) // keep 3 remove 1
```

`kept == 0` lets the first interval in. `>=` keeps intervals that only touch, like `{2, 3}` and `{3, 4}`. Write `>` when touching counts as a clash. Intervals with the same end can sort in either order, and the count comes out the same. The sort is O(n log n), and the walk is O(n) with O(1) extra space.

